16:14:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re spot on — that’s a huge practical flaw I glossed over! Even if we completely ignored pollution and emissions, **glass, metal, ceramics, concrete, bricks, and many other materials simply won’t burn**. They don’t combust; at best, they melt at extremely high temperatures, but they don’t turn into gas or disappear. In a real incinerator: - **Glass** just melts and can fuse into a gooey mess that clogs grates and equipment. - **Metal cans** (aluminum, steel) also melt into a molten puddle, but they don’t burn. They often survive as lumps in the leftover ash and have to be fished out afterward, which is inefficient. - **Inert stuff** like dirt, rocks, ceramics, and construction debris just sits there taking up space and heat without contributing any fuel. So if you literally tried to “burn all our trash,” you’d end up with a huge pile of non-combustible rubble and slag that would still need to be landfilled (or somehow recycled). That’s why real waste-to-energy plants typically **sort out non-combustibles first** — they pull out metals, glass, and other recyclables before burning because those materials damage equipment and reduce efficiency. Your point really underscores why burning can’t be the whole solution: a large chunk of our waste is basically fireproof. It’s one more reason the mantra **reduce, reuse, recycle (and compost)** comes before “burn” in the waste hierarchy.
